Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Simulating the Emergence of Evolutionarily Stable Reproductive and Parenting \
Strategies using Agent-Based Modeling with Evolutionary Game Theory.\
\>", "Section",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9326404307454863`*^9, 3.932640454781645*^9}, 
   3.9782721742741413`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"9ff44e61-1234-4369-a96f-\
685b19cb4dce"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["The Problem", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9326405424095573`*^9, 
  3.932640546337922*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"2d633ecd-11f7-4667-af10-\
affc244dd073"],

Cell["\<\
\tThe processes by which life interacts with its environment, sustains \
itself, and continues on through its posterity (reproduces) are complex and \
intricately shaped by environmental factors and evolution. One interesting \
aspect of organism\[CloseCurlyQuote]s behavior is the reasons why organisms \
reproduce the way they do, and how they take care of their young: their \
reproductive and parenting behaviors. Agent-Based Modeling (ABM) simulations, \
while greatly simplified, can provide interesting theory that can provide a \
basis and a framework for actual studies of ecological behaviors and traits \
in organisms. Agent-Based Modeling simulations consist of agents that \
interact with each other and their environment through simple rules. As the \
simulation evolves through time, the rules produce interesting behavior among \
the agents that can be studied. These rules ideally mimic real life to create \
a realistic, albeit simplified, simulation.
\tWe would like to apply Agent-Based Modeling and Evolutionary Game Theory in \
Wolfram Language to create a testing ground for investigating the emergence \
of reproductive and parenthood strategies in organisms. Specifically, we \
imagine creating Wolfram Functions for the Wolfram Community so ourselves and \
users can run experiments on custom models we create, in which ourselves and \
the Wolfram Community can tweak environment setups, agent behavior, and other \
parameters of our model to analyze statistical data produced by the \
simulation and gain insights on reproductive and parenting strategies of \
organisms in the real world. We\[CloseCurlyQuote]re interested in answering \
deeper questions through ABM, including but not limited to:\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.966098843758082*^9, 3.966098857907597*^9}, {
   3.978284274767214*^9, 3.978284285254566*^9}, {3.9782843450693207`*^9, 
   3.9782843743160877`*^9}, {3.978284408852804*^9, 3.9782844101092873`*^9}, {
   3.9782844456391945`*^9, 3.978284486981245*^9}, {3.97828491233667*^9, 
   3.9782849163061867`*^9}, {3.9782850129969788`*^9, 3.978285017694437*^9}, {
   3.978285113840723*^9, 3.978285120208727*^9}, {3.978285446956871*^9, 
   3.9782854476368504`*^9}, 3.978285938855879*^9, {3.9782872554357147`*^9, 
   3.978287257274294*^9}, {3.9782872967506123`*^9, 
   3.978287297051605*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"28019624-a97f-495e-9ae0-\
aa5c443ae2b9"],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
Why did natural selection cause organisms to reproduce the way they do (more \
children vs. less children, sexual vs. asexual vs. both (facultative \
parthenogenesis), etc.) and how is this shaped by their environment (less \
food, more predators, etc.)? Finally, how do these behaviors emerge from \
evolution and natural selection? How long do they take to? Is it faster if we \
change the environment?\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9782851411355953`*^9, 3.978285151589863*^9}, {
   3.978285187525276*^9, 3.978285187525276*^9}, 
   3.9782852252059593`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"104b24d3-737b-194c-9bcf-\
b23bd48d3d3b"],

Cell["\<\
Which behaviors are stronger, and when? Having more children when there is \
more food?\
\>", "Subitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9782851411355953`*^9, 3.978285151589863*^9}, {
  3.9782851881611633`*^9, 
  3.9782851893808327`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"db3cfbeb-4143-554a-bf7e-\
aca1e0d1bde1"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["\<\
How did the process of parenthood emerge? Why do some organisms abandon their \
young while others guard them preciously? Why do males leave their children \
while females remain with them in some species? How does this relate to their \
reproductive strategy and their environment?\
\>", "Item",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9782851411355953`*^9, 3.978285151589863*^9}, {
  3.9782851881611633`*^9, 
  3.9782851986317005`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"be7ef2b2-0a70-964b-9cc2-\
888480283571"],

Cell["\<\
Which parenting strategies are the strongest based on an organism\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s environment? Have many offspring and abandon them? Guard \
them preciously? Feed them, but only a little bit, saving most resources for \
yourself? What if there are predators, what if there aren\[CloseCurlyQuote]t?\
\
\>", "Subitem",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.9782851411355953`*^9, 3.978285151589863*^9}, {
  3.9782851881611633`*^9, 
  3.9782852196738834`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"b9cabab0-5d7a-344f-9478-\
275f09050dc1"]
}, Open  ]]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Implementation", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.932640553940339*^9, 3.932640558345264*^9}, {
  3.9782850431139336`*^9, 
  3.978285061249752*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"dbc99c96-ffab-4bf6-bf49-\
fd885fd8f33f"],

Cell[TextData[{
 "\tOur ABM approach is particularly inspired by the ",
 StyleBox["Primer",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 " YouTube channel: https://www.youtube.com/@PrimerBlobs for their insightful \
investigations of evolutionary phenomena done through ABM simulations, \
ranging from behavior to genetics. We don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t hope to \
completely mimic ",
 StyleBox["Primer",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 ", but our implementation, on a higher-level, takes heavy inspiration from \
the process with which ",
 StyleBox["Primer ",
  FontSlant->"Italic"],
 "uses to explore evolution and behavior, and he was not the first to \
implement ecological ABM in this manner. \n\tIn the Agent-Based Model where \
we may tweak parameters and collect data on the simulation over time. The \
simulation would be complete with visuals and animations, showing what\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s going on behind the data collection. Behavior is \
deterministic and always based on an agent\[CloseCurlyQuote]s genes. It is \
important to note that agents do not \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]feel\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] or are not provided \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]rewards\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote] by the simulation. Rather, natural selection drives \
some agents to do better than others, causing other agents to die out, \
showing how some strategies are advantageous.\n\tAgents represent organisms \
with age and energy, must consume resources to survive, move, reproduce, and \
optionally invest energy in offspring. Two core genes determine behavior: one \
controlling reproductive output (number of offspring) and one controlling \
parenting decisions. Agents reproduce [sexually/asexually] once energy \
thresholds are met, and mix genes (and therefore behavior) for their child \
through a simplified haploid gene mixing. Parenting strategies range from \
complete neglect to unconditional care, conditional care based on child \
hunger, or favoritism among offspring. Children are more vulnerable than \
adults, which creates evolutionary pressure for parenting behaviors. \
Parenting (feeding children) interactions are framed using evolutionary game \
theory, with payoff matrices determining outcomes based on child wellbeing \
and parenting decisions. Evolutionary stable strategies can be determined \
through empirical gene success coupled with theoretical Strong/Weak Nash \
Equilibrium analysis.\n\tThe environment supplies food with adjustable \
abundance and variability, including seasonal fluctuations that can stress \
different strategies. Energy dynamics govern all actions, including baseline \
metabolism, movement, reproduction, and parenting."
}], "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.966098861106164*^9, 3.9660988700286694`*^9}, {
  3.978284447842451*^9, 3.978284454659092*^9}, {3.9782854486983776`*^9, 
  3.9782854500574455`*^9}, {3.9782855672200146`*^9, 3.978285582918108*^9}, {
  3.9782856313012447`*^9, 3.978285650135044*^9}, {3.9782858681501656`*^9, 
  3.9782858742936497`*^9}, {3.9782859053785744`*^9, 3.9782859183349743`*^9}, {
  3.978285963258684*^9, 3.9782860033182583`*^9}, {3.9782869195315742`*^9, 
  3.978287042956291*^9}, {3.9782871055696507`*^9, 3.9782871286609097`*^9}, {
  3.9782872095041847`*^9, 3.978287252279907*^9}, {3.9782873964696617`*^9, 
  3.9782874094696617`*^9}, {3.978287543707691*^9, 3.978287584508379*^9}, {
  3.9782876177305965`*^9, 3.9782876414081554`*^9}, {3.9782885750752945`*^9, 
  3.978288637932783*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"a1743c4c-53d3-421c-ad3d-\
9cfe03a71731"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Team Fit", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.932640560505062*^9, 3.932640568409265*^9}, {
  3.978285050748455*^9, 
  3.9782850513526897`*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"11587d63-f329-4a0d-8997-\
4712cabf95c2"],

Cell["\<\
\tAs a group, we have found the emergence of evolutionary behaviors and \
traits of organisms through the process of natural selection fascinating. We\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]ve got plenty of the math and programming experience to \
build a custom ABM simulation, and the wonderful thing about creating a \
custom ABM simulation is that we can control its complexity and make sure \
that the scope of the project fits our timeframes, skills, and \
investigations/experiments we would like to conduct. 
\tMuch biological theory exists and explains reproductive and parenting \
strategies (I can talk about it but don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t have the space to \
put it on this proposal). However, we hope to use Agent-Based Modeling to \
create Wolfram tools with which to explore parenting and reproductive \
behaviors free of the limitations of real world where ourselves and our users \
can make the simulation their own. Here, we can observe these behaviors \
emerge naturally instead of viewing it through the lens of pre-established \
biological theory, and analyze not only what causes these parenting and \
reproductive behaviors, but also the nature of their emergence and how they \
are shaped by rapidly changing environmental factors.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.96609887330823*^9, 3.9660988804103107`*^9}, {
   3.9782841700032063`*^9, 3.978284189663044*^9}, {3.978284225486843*^9, 
   3.978284299142044*^9}, {3.978284615629341*^9, 3.9782846320492077`*^9}, {
   3.978285259239088*^9, 3.978285367956686*^9}, 3.978285450953863*^9, {
   3.978285564955719*^9, 3.9782855878887997`*^9}, {3.9782874303228035`*^9, 
   3.9782874310195236`*^9}, {3.9782884756291027`*^9, 3.978288504358036*^9}, {
   3.9785266222664433`*^9, 
   3.978526622942812*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"1893e66e-7f92-422a-b440-\
2b5200fc99c6"]
}, Open  ]],

Cell[CellGroupData[{

Cell["Data", "Subsection",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.932640589168119*^9, 3.93264059989354*^9}, {
  3.9782850544566975`*^9, 
  3.978285054788063*^9}},ExpressionUUID->"e45b968e-6137-430f-a692-\
80a960439f1c"],

Cell["\<\
\tWe will not need prior data, and our simulation will create data. Our \
rulesets will not be formed off of data analysis or running machine learning \
or regression models on biological data, but instead from conceptual \
biological theory. For example, biology says that when gametes unite in \
fertilization, haploid nuclei fuse to form a diploid organism. When we \
implement sexual reproduction in our simulation, based on this biological \
concept, we would have genes in our simulation combine in a similar manner. \
One implementation of this that comes to mind with is having a child\
\[CloseCurlyQuote]s gene be an average of the two parents\[CloseCurlyQuote] \
gene, whatever they\[CloseCurlyQuote]re modeling, like size or speed. We \
actually don\[CloseCurlyQuote]t plan to introduce variation in size or speed \
in favor of exploring reproductive behaviors and parenting since that type of \
work has been done before, but those are examples.\
\>", "Text",
 CellChangeTimes->{{3.93264060127557*^9, 3.932640667131102*^9}, {
   3.9782723574970665`*^9, 3.9782723917490864`*^9}, {3.9785286862309856`*^9, 
   3.9785287157885704`*^9}, {3.978528816697071*^9, 3.9785288785224705`*^9}, {
   3.978529016946148*^9, 3.9785293934856873`*^9}, {3.9785296219651737`*^9, 
   3.978529622865526*^9}, 
   3.9785526583829365`*^9},ExpressionUUID->"50a15e06-d75e-4737-b525-\
2b0c1d788ca2"]
}, Open  ]],

